@@ -0,0 +1,114 @@
+# accounts
+
+A command-line tool that manages local user accounts with passwords, kept in a
+single store file. Python 3.11+, standard library only.
+
+## Usage
+
+```
+PYTHONPATH=src python -m accounts --store PATH COMMAND USERNAME
+```
+
+The store is the file at `PATH`; it is created on first use and persists
+between runs. Passwords are never passed as command-line arguments — each
+command reads the passwords it needs from standard input, one per line.
+
+| Command            | Reads from stdin                | On success               |
+| ------------------ | ------------------------------- | ------------------------ |
+| `register USERNAME`| new password                     | `registered USERNAME`    |
+| `login USERNAME`   | password                         | `ok`                     |
+| `unlock USERNAME`  | nothing                          | `unlocked USERNAME`      |
+| `passwd USERNAME`  | current password, then new one   | `changed password for …`  |
+| `delete USERNAME`  | password                         | `deleted USERNAME`       |
+
+A username is 3–32 characters of letters, digits and underscores. A password
+must be at least 12 characters.
+
+## Exit statuses
+
+| Status | Meaning                                                   |
+| ------ | --------------------------------------------------------- |
+| 0      | success                                                   |
+| 1      | `invalid credentials` (wrong password)                    |
+| 2      | invalid username, password too short, or bad usage        |
+| 3      | no such user, or `register` of a name that already exists |
+| 4      | `account locked`                                          |
+| 5      | the store is missing a parent it cannot create, unreadable, or corrupt |
+
+Error messages go to standard error; `invalid credentials` and
+`account locked` are written verbatim, as specified.
+
+## Lockout
+
+Each failed `login` increments the account's failed-attempt counter. The fifth
+consecutive failure locks the account; that fifth attempt still reports
+`invalid credentials` (status 1), and every later `login` reports
+`account locked` (status 4) — even with the correct password. A successful
+`login` resets the counter, and `unlock` clears both the lock and the counter.
+
+## Examples
+
+```console
+$ printf 'correct horse battery\n' | python -m accounts --store users.db register alice
+registered alice
+$ printf 'correct horse battery\n' | python -m accounts --store users.db login alice
+ok
+$ printf 'wrong password!!\n' | python -m accounts --store users.db login alice
+invalid credentials          # on stderr, exit status 1
+$ printf 'short\n' | python -m accounts --store users.db register bob
+password too short: at least 12 characters required   # stderr, exit status 2
+```
+
+## Layout
+
+```
+src/accounts/__main__.py    python -m accounts entry point
+src/accounts/cli.py         argument parsing, stdin handling, exit statuses
+src/accounts/service.py     register / login / unlock / passwd / delete, validation, lockout
+src/accounts/passwords.py   scrypt hashing and constant-time verification
+src/accounts/store.py       the JSON store file: tolerant reads, atomic writes
+src/accounts/errors.py      exit statuses and the error type carrying them
+tests/test_accounts.py      end-to-end tests driving python -m accounts
+```
+
+## Tests
+
+```
+PYTHONPATH=src python -m unittest discover -s tests -v
+```
+
+## Notes on how the store works
+
+Passwords are stored as salted `hashlib.scrypt` hashes (16-byte random salt per
+account, n=2^14, r=8, p=1, 32-byte key) and compared with
+`hmac.compare_digest`. Each record keeps its own salt and cost parameters, so
+the parameters can be raised later without invalidating existing accounts.
+
+The store is a JSON object (`{"version": 1, "users": {…}}`) written with
+owner-only permissions (0600). Writes go to a temporary file in the same
+directory, are flushed and fsynced, then renamed over the target, so an
+interrupted run cannot leave a half-written store. A missing or empty file
+reads as an empty store; a corrupt one is reported rather than silently reset.
+
+## Judgment calls where the specification is silent
+
+- `login` for a username that does not exist reports `invalid credentials`
+  (status 1) rather than "no such user", so the tool does not disclose which
+  usernames are registered. Status 3 is used for the non-existent user in
+  `unlock`, `passwd` and `delete`, as specified. An unknown-user login also
+  computes a throwaway hash, so its timing resembles a wrong-password login.
+- Username validation (status 2) is applied to `register`. For the other
+  commands an unusable name simply does not exist in the store, so it is
+  reported as no such user (status 3).
+- Only failed `login` attempts count toward the lockout; a wrong current
+  password in `passwd` or `delete` does not lock the account, and a lock does
+  not block those two commands when the correct password is supplied.
+- In `passwd`, the current password is verified before the new password's
+  length is checked — if both are wrong, the result is status 1.
+- End of input where a password is expected is treated as an empty password,
+  which the usual rules then reject: status 2 for `register` and for the new
+  password in `passwd`, status 1 for `login` and `delete`.
+- A password is taken verbatim apart from its line ending, so leading and
+  trailing spaces are significant; a trailing `\r` from CRLF input is removed.
+- `unlock`, `passwd` and `delete` print a short confirmation line; only the
+  `registered USERNAME` and `ok` outputs are fixed by the specification.
